  "chooseSection": {
    "heading": "How to Choose a Copilot Money Alternative",
    "body": "Selecting an alternative to Copilot Money comes down to five operational points: device platforms, household collaboration, budgeting methodology, trial length, and annual commitment.\n\nFirst, review platform availability. Copilot Money's pricing page lists access strictly on iPhone, iPad, Mac and Web. If someone in your household tracks finances on other operating systems, cross-platform services like Monarch, YNAB, Simplifi and EveryDollar maintain broader application support, while Tiller feeds data into private Google Sheets or Microsoft Excel spreadsheets accessible from any browser.\n\nSecond, determine whether you need to share access today. Copilot Money marks its Households sharing feature as coming soon on its pricing page. YNAB allows a single subscription to be shared among up to six people at no extra cost. Simplifi includes sharing with one other person. Monarch includes partner collaboration on its standard accounts. Selecting a provider with active multi-user support prevents paying for two separate subscriptions.\n\nThird, match the budgeting philosophy to your habits. Copilot Money tracks transactions and balances with an automated money assistant. YNAB relies on the YNAB Method, asking users what each dollar is for before spending it. EveryDollar uses a zero-based model where users give every dollar a job. Simplifi sets up a Spending Plan that isolates bills and savings first, leaving the remainder for day-to-day spending. Tiller places raw transaction data into private spreadsheets without imposing a specific budgeting model.\n\nFourth, check the risk-free evaluation terms. Copilot Money does not list a standard free trial duration on its main pricing table. In contrast, YNAB provides a 34-day free trial with no credit card required when signing up directly on its website. Tiller offers a 30-day trial where the card is not charged until the period ends, paired with a subsequent 30-day refund window that yields up to 60 days risk-free. Monarch offers a free first week with a money-back guarantee. EveryDollar provides a 14-day free trial for its Premium plan.\n\nFifth, compare total subscription costs over a full year. Copilot Money lists an annual price of $109, which is $1 more than twelve monthly payments of $9 ($108 total), though it is advertised as $10 less than the regular $119 rate. Monarch Core costs $99.99 per year, while YNAB costs $109 per year. Tiller charges $99 per year. EveryDollar Premium costs $79.99 billed annually. These fixed annual figures allow direct comparison across budgeting budgets."
  },
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "heading": "Monarch Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Monarch sells two subscription plans: Core at $99.99 per year or $14.99 per month, and Plus at $199.99 per year. Monarch offers a free first week, allows cancellation at any time, and provides a money-back guarantee. A promotional banner on Monarch's pricing page advertises 30% off Core for one year when using code WELCOME, subject to terms and expiration. Monarch states that it displays no advertisements, never sells customer financial data, and recommends completing sign-up on the web rather than through mobile app stores.\n\nMonarch Core includes account tracking, budgeting, and shared views for couples. Monarch Plus adds forecasting tools, tracking for multiple income sources such as a small business, and deeper investment tracking features.\n\nMonarch fits households and partners who want shared budgeting, net worth tracking, and optional multi-income business tracking without commercial advertisements.\n\nVersus Copilot Money, Monarch costs less on an annual Core plan but more on a monthly plan. Copilot Money charges $9 per month or $109 billed yearly. Monarch Core costs $14.99 per month, which totals $179.88 across 12 monthly payments. Comparing annual plans, Monarch Core at $99.99 is $9.01 cheaper per year than Copilot Money at $109. However, upgrading to Monarch Plus at $199.99 per year costs $90.99 more than Copilot Money's $109 annual rate. Where Monarch falls short: Copilot Money charges a lower monthly fee ($9 versus $14.99), and Copilot Money includes its money assistant on its single plan without requiring an upgraded tier."
    },
    {
      "heading": "YNAB Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "YNAB (You Need A Budget) provides a proactive budgeting system with two billing options. Its Annual plan costs $109 paid annually, which equals $9.08 per month and is advertised as saving $70 compared to monthly billing. Its Monthly plan costs $14.99 per month. All prices are listed in United States (US) dollars plus applicable sales tax. YNAB includes a 34-day free trial that requires no credit card when signing up directly on the YNAB website, though app store sign-ups may require payment details. College students can access a free 365-day trial upon providing proof of enrollment. A single YNAB subscription can be shared with a group of up to six people at no additional charge.\n\nThe YNAB Method requires users to assign every dollar a job, asking what each dollar needs to do before the next paycheck arrives. The platform includes debt payoff tools with an integrated loan calculator to project repayment timelines.\n\nYNAB fits budgeters who want a disciplined zero-based allocation system and households that want to share one subscription across up to six individuals.\n\nVersus Copilot Money, YNAB charges the exact same annual price of $109, while offering broader multi-user sharing. Copilot Money's pricing page lists Households as coming soon, whereas YNAB already supports sharing with up to six people under its standard subscription. On monthly billing, Copilot Money is cheaper at $9 per month ($108 over 12 months) compared to YNAB's $14.99 monthly rate ($179.88 over 12 months, a difference of $70.88). Where YNAB falls short: users who prefer passive tracking with a money assistant may find YNAB's hands-on dollar-allocation method requires more daily maintenance than Copilot Money's interface."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Simplifi Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Simplifi is a cloud budgeting application for web and mobile devices, developed by Quicken. The Simplifi homepage displays pricing figures of $3.99 per month and $6.99 labeled billed annually. A footnote explains that discounts are calculated on the annual price, the final price paid may differ from the monthly figure multiplied by 12, promotional offers apply only to the first year for new memberships ordered directly by December 8, 2026, and subscriptions renew automatically at the then-current standard rates. Simplifi states that its services are not designed to function outside the US and Canada.\n\nSimplifi connects to over 14,000 financial institutions. Its feature set includes a Spending Plan that prioritizes bills and savings goals before displaying available discretionary funds, Projected Cash Flow looking up to 12 months into the future, net worth tracking, Watchlist categories, Savings Goals, a Retirement Planner, Quicken AI, free one-on-one setup help from customer specialists, and the ability to share account access with one other person.\n\nSimplifi fits individuals and couples who want forward-looking cash flow projections, retirement planning tools, and expert onboarding assistance.\n\nVersus Copilot Money, Simplifi offers forward-looking cash flow projections up to 12 months ahead and active two-person sharing, whereas Copilot Money lists Households as coming soon. Where Simplifi falls short: Simplifi restricts its product design to the US and Canada, includes promotional terms that renew at higher ongoing rates, and does not provide Copilot Money's single-price model without renewal footnotes."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Tiller Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "Tiller is a personal finance service that synchronizes daily financial transactions into private spreadsheets. Tiller offers a 30-day free trial, followed by an annual subscription price of $99 per year, with sales tax added where required by law. Tiller states that its service carries no advertisements and no hidden fees. New subscribers register using a Microsoft or Google account, and credit cards are not charged until the initial 30-day trial concludes. Tiller pairs this with an additional 30-day money-back refund guarantee, providing up to 60 days of risk-free evaluation.\n\nTiller automatically imports cleared bank transactions and account balances into private Google Sheets or Microsoft Excel workbooks that the user owns. Because the data lives in personal spreadsheets, users retain complete control over category design, formulas, and visual reporting.\n\nTiller fits spreadsheet enthusiasts who want automated bank feeds into Google Sheets or Excel without manual file exports.\n\nVersus Copilot Money, Tiller costs $99 per year, which is $10 less than Copilot Money's $109 annual rate. On a monthly equivalent basis, Tiller's $99 annual fee equals $8.25 per month, compared to Copilot Money's $9 monthly subscription. Tiller also allows unlimited customization through custom formulas. Where Tiller falls short: Tiller does not offer dedicated mobile applications like Copilot Money's iPhone and iPad apps, does not include an interactive money assistant, and does not provide an out-of-the-box mobile dashboard for rapid expense review on the go."
    },
    {
      "heading": "EveryDollar Pricing, Plans and Fit",
      "body": "EveryDollar, developed by Ramsey Solutions, is a budgeting application centered on zero-based budgeting principles. EveryDollar offers a permanent Free version that includes monthly budget creation, unlimited budget categories, manual transaction entry, sinking funds, split transactions, and tracking for bill due dates.\n\nEveryDollar Premium adds automated bank connectivity, custom budget reports, Comma-Separated Values (CSV) transaction exports, live Question and Answer (Q&A) sessions with financial coaches, and net worth projection modeling. Following a 14-day free trial, EveryDollar Premium costs $79.99 billed annually (listed as $6.67 per month equivalent), or $17.99 per month when billed on a monthly basis.\n\nEveryDollar fits people who want zero-based budgeting with a free manual option, or those who value live financial coach Q&A sessions on the Premium plan.\n\nVersus Copilot Money, EveryDollar provides a free tier for manual budget tracking, which Copilot Money does not offer. For automated bank syncing, EveryDollar Premium billed annually costs $79.99 per year, saving $29.01 compared to Copilot Money's $109 annual price. However, on monthly billing, EveryDollar Premium is substantially more expensive: 12 months at $17.99 totals $215.88, which is $107.88 more than 12 months of Copilot Money at $9 per month ($108 total). Where EveryDollar falls short: Copilot Money charges a much lower monthly subscription fee ($9 versus $17.99), includes an integrated money assistant, and does not require manual entry on its entry price point."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who This Is Not For",
      "body": "This comparison focuses on consumer personal budgeting and spending applications. It is not suitable for several distinct financial needs:\n\nSmall businesses requiring double-entry bookkeeping, invoicing, or payroll should not use these personal budgeting tools. Dedicated business software platforms, such as those covered in our [comparison hub](/compare/), handle general ledgers and corporate compliance.\n\nInvestors seeking active trading platforms, portfolio margin lending, or automated algorithmic order execution will not find those features in Copilot Money or these alternatives. While Monarch, Simplifi, and EveryDollar include investment or net worth tracking, they do not execute securities trades.\n\nBudgeters who insist on a permanent, zero-cost automated bank sync cannot use Copilot Money, Monarch, YNAB, or Tiller, as all four require paid subscriptions to maintain automatic bank connections. EveryDollar offers a $0 tier, but automatic bank syncing is restricted to its paid Premium plan."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Would Change Our Answer on Copilot Money Alternatives",
      "body": "The analysis on this page is based on published prices, features, and platform limits checked on November 24, 2026. Several potential moves by these providers would shift the cost calculations and recommendations:\n\nIf Copilot Money releases its Households sharing feature without adding an extra fee, its value for partners and families would rise relative to Monarch and YNAB.\n\nIf Copilot Money ends its limited-time $109 annual promotion and returns to the listed $119 price, the annual price gap between Copilot Money and Monarch Core would expand from $9.01 to $19.01, and Copilot Money would cost $10 more per year than YNAB ($119 versus $109).\n\nIf Monarch alters its Core ($99.99) or Plus ($199.99) pricing tiers, or makes its WELCOME promo code permanent, the comparative annual math would adjust accordingly.\n\nIf YNAB modifies its 34-day free trial or changes its policy allowing up to six people to share one $109 subscription, the multi-user cost advantage would shift.\n\nIf Simplifi alters the pricing or expiration date on its promotional first-year offer of $3.99 per month, or changes its standard $6.99 renewal rate, the cost comparison against Copilot Money's flat annual fee would change.\n\nIf EveryDollar adjusts its $79.99 annual or $17.99 monthly Premium pricing, the threshold at which its automated tier beats Copilot Money would move."
    }
